    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61922,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61923,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours typically occur in women of reproductive age, who present with nonspecific symptoms and signs related to a pelvic/abdominal mass, although ovarian myxoma may be an incidental finding {2032767}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61924,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are rare {23722509; 2032767}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61925,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61926,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It has been postulated that ovarian myxomas may be related to the fibroma/thecoma and/or sclerosing stromal tumour category of tumours, because some myxomas have been reported to show transition to sclerosing stromal tumour or thecoma {7688213; 25083960}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61927,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours range in size but are typically &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;cm and show a mucoid/gelatinous cut surface, sometimes punctuated by cysts {2032767}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61928,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are well circumscribed, although not encapsulated from the surrounding ovarian parenchyma. They have abundant background myxoid matrix and are paucicellular overall, but cellularity varies from area to area, with ovarian myxomas being cellular more often than their intramuscular counterparts. Spindle to stellate cells are haphazardly arranged and display long tapering cytoplasmic processes and oval to indented bland nuclei with occasional nuclear pseudoinclusions and absent to minimal mitotic activity. Small capillary-type vessels, sometimes with a plexiform arrangement, are typically seen throughout the tumour. Small cysts/pseudocysts and recent haemorrhage with or without fibrin deposition may be seen. Tumour cells are positive for vimentin and frequently for SMA, but they are negative for desmin, S100, keratins, and EMA {1996543; 7688213; 2032767}. By electron microscopy, the tumour cells show fibroblastic/myofibroblastic differentiation {1502739}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61929,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 476,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:01:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
